speechtext:
On the order: Government notices of motion:
April 23-The Minister of National Revenue- The following proposed motion;-That a select

committee be appointed on broadcasting to consider radio and television broadcasting together with the annual report of the Canadian Broadcasting Corporation, and to review the operations, policies and aims of the corporation and its revenues, expenditures and development, with power to examine and inquire into the matters herein referred to, and to report from time to time their observations and opinions thereon, and to send for persons, papers and records;
That the committee have power to print such papers and evidence from day to day as may be deemed advisable or necessary;
That the committee have power to meet while the house is sitting;
That the committee shall consist of 35 members;
That standing orders 66 and 67 be suspended in relation thereto.

Mr. Speaker, I shall do my utmost to remain within your decree. I notice there have been a large number of rulings today. It is with a little bit of difficulty that we have to debate the amendment to the supply motion which is now before the house. I would like to begin by reading into the record the amendment that is now before us, as moved by the hon. member for Ottawa West (Mr. Mcll-raith):
This house regrets the ill-conceived and irresponsible actions of the government in the field of debt management and monetary policy which have been characterized by:
1.	The errors and inequities associated with the conversion loan;
2.	The highest bank rate since the Bank of Canada was established;
3.	The largest expansion of the money supply in any year since the war;
4.	The greatest increase in peacetime in the national debt;
5.	The highest public debt charges in Canada's history.
We in our group, Mr. Speaker, admit the complaints that the Liberal spokesman raised. We admit that there have been ill-conceived and irresponsible actions on the part of the government in the field of debt management and monetary policy. It is our contention that since the days of the hungry thirties, the years 1930 to 1935, the Conservatives have learned nothing, and that all the remedies they have to offer to meet head on the problem in an attempt to reverse the trend of an ever-worsening recession in Canada are higher taxes, higher tariffs and higher interest rates. I regret very much that the Liberals did not lodge any complaint in their amendment about the lack of action. They mentioned the ill-conceived and irresponsible actions of the government in the matter of debt management and monetary policies, and we agree that whatever action has been taken by the government and the Minister of Finance (Mr. Fleming), has been irresponsible and ill-conceived. However, we very much regret that there was no proposal offered in the amendment that might have suggested a solution to the difficulty that the amendment complains about.
